WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:07.400
AI tools are security boundaries because they connect model output to systems, data, communications, or actions with real impact.

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:09.940
Read-only tools may retrieve information.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:21.680
Write-capable tools may send email, update calendars, modify code, change cloud resources, create tickets, query or update databases, or initiate payment-style workflows at a high level.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:27.500
The architecture must treat those capabilities as privileged integration points, not as harmless model features.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.680
Action controls should match the impact of the tool.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:34.820
Low-risk read-only lookups may require standard authorization and logging.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:47.520
High-impact actions need stronger controls: argument validation, allowlists, rate limits, sandboxing, approval gates, transaction previews, dry-run modes, rollback planning, and separation of duties.

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:55.580
A model can propose a tool call, but deterministic code should validate whether the request is expected, allowed, safe, and authorized.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:04.120
Approval gates are useful when an action could affect real people, money, production systems, sensitive data, or external communications.

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:11.220
The approval should show what will happen, which identity will be used, which resource will change, and what evidence supports the action.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:15.680
Approving a vague intent is weaker than approving a specific transaction preview.

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:20.540
For high-impact actions, the system should record who approved what and when.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:25.320
Agentic systems need stronger controls because the model may choose steps over time.

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:29.380
Autonomy can create value, but it also increases the importance of boundaries.

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:36.800
Agents should have scoped goals, limited tools, observable plans, interrupt points, and clear rollback or containment options.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:42.540
The more an AI system can do, the more architecture must constrain and monitor what it is allowed to do.
